  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#activation has existed in one form or another since the initial check-in of a document called Web Applications 1.0. That was back in 2006. In 2008 it briefly came up on the mailing list here: https://lists.whatwg.org/pipermail/whatwg-whatwg.org/2008-July/057572.html. I can't find any other discussion.\n\nMy reading of the specification today is that the coordinates for such events are most likely their initial values (zero), although the `MouseEvent` specification and `PointerEvent` specification do not make those particularly clear.\n\nWe have found that zero is bad for accessibility as menus and such can end up being positioned in the wrong place and as such WebKit uses the midpoint of the smallest rectangle that contains the target element in question (think `getBoundingClientRect()`).\n\nWe have considered aligning with other engines on zero, but this seems bad as it makes assistive technology more discoverable and would also make it even more likely for websites to do the wrong thing. So what we'd really like to do is clarify the specification on this and require the midpoint of the target element to be used.\n\ncc @whatwg/a11y ",
